    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The volatility is amplified because a Green New Deal often mandates a forced, \"just\" transition that demands rapid sector 1 consolidation which can crash valuations before new green assets fully mature. Unlike a typical market correction where investors can exit gradually, the policy often locks in specific stranded asset write-downs that can overwhelm liquidity in traditional energy and manufacturing markets. This creates a \"forced liquidity event\" where capital is siphoned from legacy firms faster than it can be replaced by new green infrastructure, leading to sudden credit crunches and potential deposit outflows that can trigger systemic failure in . This structural shock is more severe than normal cyclical downturns because it is policy-driven rather than demand-driven.",
